---
// Lesson 11.1c — Create PT: Build Day 2
// hybrid CPT build day: the algorithm — sequencing + selection + iteration.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>11.1c — Create PT: Build Day 2</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Day 1 built the list (<a href="11.1b.html">11.1b</a>); today
    builds the ALGORITHM — the procedure that earns its rubric row
    by doing all three at once: sequencing (statements in order),
    selection (if), iteration (loop). This is the piece the video
    will feature and WR2a will explain. Make it proud.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity hybrid">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~50 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The algorithm contract — annotate yours to
    match:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// SEQUENCING: these lines run in THIS order, on purpose
public int ScoreHand(List&lt;Card&gt; cards, int bonus)   // ← parameters
{
    int total = 0;                                    // 1. start
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; cards.Count; i++)            // 2. ITERATION
    {
        Card c = cards[i];
        if (c.cost &lt;= 3)                             // 3. SELECTION
        {
            total += c.attack + bonus;
        }
        else
        {
            total += c.attack / 2;
        }
    }
    if (total &gt; 20) { total = 20; }                   // 4. SELECTION, again
    return total;                                     // 5. output
}</code></pre>
    <p>Why the exam loves this shape: it is not hard; it is
    COMPLETE. Sequencing is the spine, selection makes decisions,
    iteration handles many items, and the procedure takes
    PARAMETERS so callers can reuse it. Your
    <a href="10.2d.html">10.2d</a> AI score method IS this
    algorithm — you have been writing rubric answers all year
    without knowing the rubric's name.</p>

    <p><strong>Build order for the day:</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Write the procedure with parameters</strong>
            (two beats: what it processes, what it decides on).
            Name the parameters well — they appear in your
            screenshots and WR.</li>
        <li><strong>Call it from three different places</strong>
            — a call per context proves reuse, and graders read
            calls as "this is real behavior, not a demo."</li>
        <li><strong>Print before/after:</strong> Debug.Log the
            input state and output state at each call site.
            Input → behavior → output, made visible.</li>
        <li><strong>Break it on purpose</strong> (5 min): empty
            list, zero, one item, giant item. Fix what crashes.
            The PT video cannot crash — you have ONE take.</li>
    </ol>

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity hybrid">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Close of Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~5 min</span>
    </div>


    <div class="socratic">


        <h3>Socratic: Sequencing, Selection, Iteration</h3>


        <ol>


            <li>Your procedure does all three at once. Which line is the selection, and what would happen if it were deleted?</li>


            <li>A grader must be able to find the parameter and the call. Can you name both from memory without opening the file?</li>


            <li>You built this before the input and output the video will show. What is the risk in that order, and what did you do about it today?</li>


        </ol>


    </div>



    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Label S/S/I in your algorithm line by line</li>
            <li>Show three call sites with different arguments</li>
            <li>Prove the algorithm survives edge inputs</li>
            <li>Read your WR2a paragraph out loud in under 40 seconds</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../exam/create-performance-task.html">Create PT guide</a> — the algorithm rubric rows</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/written-responses.html">Written Responses</a> — how WR2a is graded</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="11.1d.html">11.1d — Create PT: Build Day 3</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="11.1c" title="11.1c Create PT: Build Day 2 — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
